Facts & Conditionals

Today I learned about Facts & Conditionals in Ansible, which help in making automation intelligent and environment-aware 🤖
Instead of running the same task everywhere, Ansible can make decisions based on system information.
Smart automation = Conditional automation ⚡

🔹 📊 Ansible Facts
Ansible Facts are system information automatically collected from managed nodes.
These include:
 • OS name
 • IP address
 • Hostname
 • Memory details
 • CPU information
Facts help Playbooks understand the target system before executing tasks.

🔹 🔍 Gathering Facts
By default, Ansible gathers facts at the start of a Playbook using:
 gather_facts: yes

Example:
- hosts: all
 gather_facts: yes
This allows Ansible to use system data during execution.
 You can also disable it to speed up execution if not needed 🚀

🔹 ⚙️ when Conditions
The when condition is used to run tasks only when a specific condition is true.
Example:
- name: Install nginx on Ubuntu
 apt:
 name: nginx
 state: present
 when: ansible_os_family == "Debian"
This makes Playbooks dynamic and decision-based instead of static.

🔹 🖥️ OS-Based Tasks
Using facts + when conditions, we can run different tasks based on the operating system.
Example logic:
 • Use apt for Ubuntu/Debian
 • Use yum for RedHat/CentOS
This ensures the same Playbook works across multiple environments 🌍
OS-based automation is widely used in real production setups.

🔥 3-Line Takeaway
Facts provide system intelligence.
 Conditions control task execution.
 Smart Playbooks adapt to any OS automatically.
